---
title: Arity is the capability test
summary: Why skoro has no capability traits or supports_* flags; a model's shape is read off its terms' bindings at run time, and coverage is checked explicitly.
order: 6
---

## Context

An estimator needs to know whether a model can give it what it needs, for example whether every
term is Gaussian-shaped or offers a Fisher root. A `GaussianModelClientOps` trait bound once
expressed "this client can add Gaussians".

## Decision

There are no capability flags, `supports_*` predicates, or capability traits. A model's shape is
read at run time from its terms and bindings. Every reader matches on the arity of a `bound` call:
`[] => skip`, `[x] => use`, `_ => Err(ShapeMismatch)`. Two complementary packagings exist:

- `GaussianBlock::of`: *is this term Gaussian-shaped?*
- `coverage::terms_missing` / `coverage::require_role`: *which scored terms can't this reader see?*

## Consequences

- A bespoke distribution can offer any subset of roles, including none, and still take part.
- Readers skip silently, so a skipped term produces a well-conditioned solve of the **wrong**
  system. `require_role` is the precondition an algorithm must assert before it assembles
  anything.
- `ModelClientOps` is not dyn-compatible; every consumer is statically generic over it.

> [!DECISION] The quote that settled it
> What the old `GaussianModelClientOps` bound expressed ("this client can add Gaussians") was
> never a claim about what a model *contained*, and every client that can grow a model can add one.
